 "verdict": "RUNG (the raw arm reproduces argmax_census_instruct): 6 models compared. Identical. ANTI-VACUITY: 2 of 6 models move less than max(0.042, 2x their own seed noise) across the ENTIRE domain axis -- Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct (span 0.010, seed noise 0.021); stablelm-zephyr-3b (span 0.010, seed noise 0.021). Excluded from the PRIMARY: a flat sequence is neither monotone nor non-monotone, and scoring it either way would be vacuous. SECONDARY (EXPLORATORY -- added mid-run, after the first two models showed fixed_point_fraction pinned at the floor, so it is NOT pre-registered and its threshold was chosen with the first values already visible; it generates a hypothesis, it does not test one), distinct greedy endpoints along the same axis, read for every model including those the PRIMARY excludes -- a model pinned at zero fixed points can still reorganise: Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct [raw(0):10.5, bos(1):7.5, text_matched(35):14.5, chat_template(35):15.0]; stablelm-zephyr-3b [raw(0):4.0, bos(1):15.0, text_matched(13):1.0, chat_template(13):45.0]; SmolLM2-1.7B-Instruct [raw(0):4.0, bos(1):5.5, text_matched(30):4.5, chat_template(30):10.5]; gemma-1.1-2b-it [raw(0):15.0, bos(1):8.5, text_matched(9):16.0, chat_template(9):14.5]; Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct [raw(0):34.5, bos(1):15.0, text_matched(29):12.0, chat_template(29):16.0]; Falcon3-3B-Instruct [raw(0):3.5, bos(1):13.5, text_matched(11):13.5, chat_template(11):4.0]. Moves beyond seed noise on 4 of 6 models. So the domain reaches models whose fixed-point fraction cannot show it, and the floor cohort is not evidence of domain-invariance. STABILITY: 0 of 24 (model, domain) censuses disagree across seeds, so every class below is stable. PRIMARY, is fixed_point_fraction monotone in prefix LENGTH (judged up to each model's own tolerance, the larger of one census start and its seed noise)? SmolLM2-1.7B-Instruct [raw(0):0.979, bos(1):0.938, text_matched(30):0.000, chat_template(30):0.010] tol=0.021 monotone; gemma-1.1-2b-it [raw(0):0.948, bos(1):0.005, text_matched(9):1.000, chat_template(9):0.000] tol=0.010 NOT monotone; Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct [raw(0):0.000, bos(1):0.057, text_matched(29):0.016, chat_template(29):0.021] tol=0.010 NOT monotone; Falcon3-3B-Instruct [raw(0):0.693, bos(1):0.141, text_matched(11):0.000, chat_template(11):0.839] tol=0.156 NOT monotone. Monotone in 1 of 4 models that can carry a shape (2 excluded above). At least one model is non-monotone beyond its own tolerance, so the domain effect is NOT a length effect in general: prefix length does not order the fixed-point structure, and a length-based correction would be wrong. How COMMON the exception is needs more models -- 1 of 4 monotone here is a count, not a rate. CONTRAST at IDENTICAL length -- prose vs the model's own chat template. On fixed_point_fraction, read only for the models that cleared anti-vacuity (two arms both pinned at the floor cannot disagree): SmolLM2-1.7B-Instruct (30 tok) text 0.000 vs template 0.010; gemma-1.1-2b-it (9 tok) text 1.000 vs template 0.000; Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (29 tok) text 0.016 vs template 0.021; Falcon3-3B-Instruct (11 tok) text 0.000 vs template 0.839. They differ by >= 0.10 on 2 of 4, so the KIND of prefix matters beyond its length. On ENDPOINTS (the exploratory arm, same caveat as the SECONDARY), read for every model: Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct (35 tok) text 14.5 vs template 15.0; stablelm-zephyr-3b (13 tok) text 1.0 vs template 45.0; SmolLM2-1.7B-Instruct (30 tok) text 4.5 vs template 10.5; gemma-1.1-2b-it (9 tok) text 16.0 vs template 14.5; Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (29 tok) text 12.0 vs template 16.0; Falcon3-3B-Instruct (11 tok) text 13.5 vs template 4.0. BOUNDARY: 6 instruction-tuned models, ONE prose sample (gate1's CORPUS, reused rather than chosen for this run), one template per model, 96 starts, two census seeds. 'Kind' is three points -- special token, prose, chat markup -- not a taxonomy of prefixes, and prose truncated mid-sentence is its own oddity.",
